    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Problem-space attacks must meet the following criteria:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>(i) available transformations (realizability), using only modifications an attacker could perform in practice;</li>
        <li>(ii) preserved semantics, the transformations performed must preserve the original malicious behavior of the sample;</li>
        <li>(iii) plausibility, the sample should appear realistic upon manual inspection;</li>
        <li>(iv) robustness to preprocessing, the modifications should survive a realistic pre-processing pipeline.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The most established way to satisfy these constraints for problem-space Android malware attacks is <span class="term">gadget extraction and transplantation</span>, where self-contained code fragments alongside their known features are harvested from benign applications and transplanted into malware. As a result of gadgets being harvested from real applications, they are realizable and plausible by construction. Furthermore, since gadget transplantation only adds benign code, and thus features (0 → 1), it preserves the original behavior (semantics) of the malware.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Three major limitations</h3>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Per-sample optimization</h4><p>Existing works optimize individual samples against a single target classifier and feature space, relying on confidence scores and search heuristics or greedy selection. As a result, these attacks optimize on a per-sample basis without retaining knowledge from past evasions, capping their query efficiency and limiting their transferability to new targets.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Capability set and strategy conflated</h4><p>Existing literature often conflates two separate components of problem-space attacks: (i) the capability set, which defines modifications available to the attacker that can be made while preserving problem-space constraints; and, (ii) the strategy, which selects from the capability set in order to modify a sample to evade a target.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>A single temporal snapshot</h4><p>These approaches are evaluated only on a single temporal snapshot, ignoring the impact that temporal drift and targets that employ Active Learning (AL) have on the evasion of malware samples.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>We therefore lack a comprehensive understanding of how evasion strategies generalize and transfer across classifiers, feature spaces, and time. To address these limitations, we propose REPLICANT: a unified Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) framework that learns problem-space evasion policies.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="threat-model">
    <h2>Threat Model</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The goal of the attacker is to violate the integrity of the system by modifying malware to be classified as goodware while preserving its malicious behavior. Furthermore, in the process of achieving this goal, the attack aims to minimize the number of both malware modifications and target queries.</p>
      <p>In the <span class="term">black-box</span> setting, the attacker has no explicit knowledge of the target classifier clf<sub>target</sub> and receives only its predicted label (i.e., either benign or malicious), rather than a confidence score. To learn an evasion policy, the attacker instead uses a surrogate classifier clf<sub>surrogate</sub> created by themselves or others, which is trained on distinctly different data from the target. Therefore, the surrogate may also differ from the target in both classifier architecture or feature space.</p>
    </div>

