    <h3>Reward Function</h3>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>R<sub>success</sub> = H</h4><p>R<sub>success</sub> ≫ 0 rewards successful evasion.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>R<sub>detected</sub> = −1</h4><p>R<sub>detected</sub> ≤ 0 penalizes detection.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>R<sub>modify</sub> = −1</h4><p>R<sub>modify</sub>(s<sub>t</sub>, c<sub>m<sub>t</sub></sub>) &lt; 0 penalizes each capability applied.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>The relative magnitudes of these three terms determine the trade-offs between modification and query efficiency learned by the agent. These values were chosen to: (i) ensure every successful episode has a positive return, since the total penalties over an episode of at most H steps cannot surpass R<sub>success</sub>; and (ii) incentivize an efficient but balanced use of both modifications and queries.</p>
      <p>During training, the query policy is masked to force periodic submits (PS). We scale the reward of these forced submits by λ<sub>PS</sub> ∈ [0, 1]. We set λ<sub>PS</sub> = 0 for two reasons. First, early in training the agent is usually detected when it queries and can learn to avoid querying altogether; making these periodic queries zero-cost encourages it to explore querying. Second, once the agent is more capable, λ<sub>PS</sub> = 0 encourages the agent to voluntarily submit early to maximize reward.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>REPLICANT Agent</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A shared MLP encoder feeds three heads, each adding one further layer before its output: a query head over {'{'}SUBMIT, MODIFY{'}'}, a masked modify head over the capability set C, and a critic head estimating state value V(s<sub>t</sub>). Training uses Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), with the query policy loss applied at every step; the modify policy loss applied only on MODIFY steps, relying on the evasion reward being propagated through advantage estimation; and the critic is shared across both. The query and modify heads share a common entropy coefficient, encouraging exploration over both when to query and what to add.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip"><span class="eyebrow">A policy, not a sample</span><p>Because training yields a policy π<sub>θ</sub> rather than a fixed adversarial sample, what REPLICANT learns is not bound to a single input or target: the same π<sub>θ</sub> can be used to evade malware it has never seen and transfers to classifiers and feature spaces it was never trained against.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="setup">
    <h2>Experimental Setup</h2>
    <div class="cards two" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Hypercube dataset, 2021–2024</h4><p>In our experiments we use the Hypercube Android malware dataset, spanning 224,965 samples from 2021–2024, constructed to follow the current best practices for unbiased dataset creation: sampling from a single market (Google Play) using publication timestamps, at a statistically representative size, with a realistic malware distribution of 10% and a VirusTotal Threshold of two (VTT2).</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Seven classifiers</h4><p>We evaluate against the following seven classifiers: Linear SVM, SecSVM, Random Forest, DeepDrebin, RAMDA, HCC, and <a href="/papers/drmd/">DRMD</a>. We chose these seven classifiers because they span a diverse set of design principles, from margin maximization and ensemble voting to reconstruction, contrastive, and reward-weighted losses.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Three feature spaces</h4><p>In total, the Drebin, APIGraph, and RAMDA feature spaces contain 10,000, 11,912, and 379 features, respectively. We chose these three spaces for their differing sizes and overlap, which allow us to evaluate transferability across varying degrees of alignment between the surrogate and target feature spaces.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Training sets: two periods, ten seeds</h4><p>We repeat every experiment: (i) over ten random seeds to account for variance; and (ii) over two temporally distinct training periods (2021 &amp; 2022) to avoid bias from temporal luck. For each seed, we perform a random stratified split to produce two equal sized training sets per training year: split a trains the surrogate and REPLICANT policies, and split b trains the target (e.g., 2021a-vs-2021b and 2022a-vs-2022b).</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Testing sets: the year after training</h4><p>We evaluate attack performance using malware from the year after target training (e.g., 2023 for a 2022b target); which realistically mimics evasion by malware authors as they attempt to evade deployed detectors over time. For each seed and feature space, the evasion test set is the malware that every classifier correctly detects ensuring that differences in ASR reflect the attack rather than which malware each classifier happens to detect.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Metrics: ASR and AvgQ</h4><p>We report ASR, specifically the percentage of malware that evaded detection at a fixed per sample budget of twenty queries, as our primary metric. We also report the average number of queries per sample (AvgQ) as a secondary metric for attacks to measure query-efficiency.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Capability Set</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>REPLICANT is deliberately agnostic to what the problem-space capabilities are: it learns effective evasion policies given any capability set and a representation that captures how the modifications affect the sample. Our capability set comprises gadgets harvested by prior problem-space work, together with their feature-space effects we computed. Each gadget affects only a small set of features, so for each feature space we use the subset that changes at least one of its features, resulting in 3,143 for Drebin, 4,804 for APIGraph, and 1,739 for RAMDA. For cross-feature-space attacks, the attacker selects from the capability set applicable in their own feature space, and the target sees the effect of applying those gadgets in its own feature space.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div>
        <h4>Prior attacks</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>When both components are proposed together and not evaluated in isolation, it is unclear if the new attack has discovered a better strategy for evasion, or if a potentially sub-optimal strategy is being carried by a stronger capability set.</li>
          <li>This ambiguity is a form of the <a href="/papers/sok/">gain attribution pitfall</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="pick">
        <h4>This evaluation</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>All attacks are performed under the same threat model and capability set for each setting, to ensure reported differences reflect attack strategy rather than access to different capabilities.</li>
          <li>We also evaluate Rnd, which uses the REPLICANT framework but selects actions uniformly at random, to isolate the performance gains of policy learning.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">The ASRs we report are relative to the capability set and dataset used rather than an absolute upper bound on problem-space evasion.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="results">
    <h2>Black-Box Evasion</h2>

    <div class="stats quad">
      <div class="stat"><b class="red">78.8%</b><span>of malware evaded within twenty queries, across all 1,764 settings</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>20.9%–39.2%</b><span>improvement, beating every Android baseline by 13.6–22.2 points</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>7.4</b><span>average queries per sample across all settings, versus the 9.3 of APG</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>96.6%</b><span>mean ASR in the matched setting, using an average of 3.6 queries per sample</span></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Matched Setting</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In the <strong>matched setting</strong>, the surrogate and target classifiers share an architecture and feature space but are trained on separate training sets; with no difference between architecture or feature space, this setting represents the best-case scenario for a black-box attacker. REPLICANT achieves a mean ASR of 96.6% using an average of 3.6 queries per sample. The strongest baseline, APG, reaches only 77.7% while the ADZ, EvD, and random baselines sit at 61.5%–69.7%. APG is statistically tied with REPLICANT on SVM, SecSVM and DeepDrebin, where ranking capabilities by their contribution to the surrogate decision function most closely aligns with the target boundary. The gap is largest on the RL-based DRMD, where APG collapses to 25.2% against the 90.2% of REPLICANT, followed by RAMDA and RF (35.4 &amp; 27.6 points), with every other baseline statistically beaten on every target.</p>
    </div>

