    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Temporal Gap.</strong> Matching the classifier and feature space but training the surrogate and target in different periods, PTE retains a mean ASR of 96.6%, statistically indistinguishable from the matched setting. Therefore, the learned policy is effectively invariant to the temporal gap; the only appreciable difference is between 2021b and 2022b targets, which is a result of temporal luck rather than the surrogate-target gap.</li>
        <li><strong>Architecture Gap.</strong> When the surrogate and target share the same feature space but differ in architecture, transfer remains strong: PTE evades 87.4% of malware within twenty queries against 44.7% for STE, and overtakes STE within roughly two queries. Given a shared representation, the learned effect that each modify action has on the feature space still holds for the target; the agent, therefore, retains an accurate model of how its capabilities change a sample. The architecture gap is thus paid foremost in queries: ASR rises slowly against the targets whose boundaries differ most from the surrogate, most notably DRMD, the only RL-trained detector, whose categorically different training regime makes its boundary the least similar to the others.</li>
        <li><strong>Representation Gap.</strong> Keeping the architecture fixed while moving the surrogate and target into different feature spaces lowers ASR to 78.8%, still far above the 42.5% of sample transfer. The performance decay concentrates on pairs involving RAMDA especially when it is used as the surrogate. A policy trained on RAMDA, the smallest feature space, and deployed against a richer target space has only learned capabilities that affect a small fraction of the target representation and must therefore rely either on that sub-region to influence the decision of the target or on incidental side-effects its capabilities produce elsewhere in the larger representation. Conversely, transferring an attack into a smaller representation, such as RAMDA, can render many of its modifications invisible to the target, as they are not captured by the representation of the target. Therefore, this reduction in ASR reflects an underlying property of the representation gap rather than any intrinsic robustness of RAMDA.</li>
        <li><strong>Combined Gap.</strong> This is the hardest regime, where the surrogate differs from the target in architecture, feature space, and training period. The costs of each transferability gap now compound: the policy pays in queries to push towards the shifted boundary and in expressiveness to compose capabilities in a representation it was not trained on, lowering the ASR of PTE to 73.0%, still far above the 41.4% of STE. DRMD is the hardest target to evade, which is further compounded by the representation gap to produce the slowest per-query ASR rise. Even so, PTE reaches an ASR of 41.1% against it, whereas STE reaches only 4.2%.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip"><span class="eyebrow">Policy over Sample Transfer</span><p>A transferred sample is optimized to pass the decision boundary of the surrogate and relies on the alignment of decision boundaries. A transferred policy instead encodes a distribution over actions conditioned on the current representation of the sample. This provides PTE with a distinct advantage that is exactly where STE fails: as surrogate and target diverge, misaligned boundaries cause sample transfer to decay, while an adaptive policy re-queries until the decision boundary is crossed. Averaged over all settings, PTE reaches 78.8% ASR against 43.3% for STE, and overtakes the ASR of STE within three queries on average.</p></div>

    <blockquote class="excerpt"><p>When the surrogate and target agree on nothing but the distribution from which their training data was drawn, what transfers best is the learning of how to evade, not specific evasive samples.</p></blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="hardening">
    <h2>Adversarial Training</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Despite a long history of proposed defenses against evasion attacks, adversarial training remains one of the few defenses that has consistently demonstrated empirical robustness. Evasion attacks highlight the weakness in a models decision-making; by retraining against these adversarial samples the model can become hardened or more robust against such attacks.</p>
      <p>The robustness gained from adversarial training depends on the quality of the attacker, so we harden with the strongest attacker from each attack type (i.e., policy-based and per-sample optimization). Therefore, we use REPLICANT for learned policies and APG for per-sample optimization to provide the most informative comparison. As we are not considering transferability, and evasion rates are similar between the feature spaces in the matched setting, we focus this analysis on the Drebin feature space because it is the most widely used representation.</p>
      <p>We also produce a white-box variant of REPLICANT (<strong>REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub></strong>) for use in AT and the robustness evaluations of the hardened targets. Architecturally, REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> drops the query head and submits after every modification, since query efficiency is not required in the white-box setting; it also uses the confidence score of the target, clf<sub>score</sub>, for rewards. Since confidence deltas can be small, we scale them by λ<sub>WB</sub> = 5 for all targets. Beyond architecture and reward, the rest of the REPLICANT formulation remains unchanged.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>AT-REPLICANT</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><div><h4>Train a fresh policy</h4><p>Each round of AT-REPLICANT trains a fresh REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> policy against the classifier being hardened.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Collect every adversarial sample</h4><p>Simultaneously collecting adversarial samples throughout training exposes the classifier to many distinct variants of every malware sample, unlike collection from an already-converged policy or via greedy composition.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Retrain the classifier</h4><p>The unique adversarial samples collected are added to the training set of the classifier for retraining before the next round.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Repeat for 15 rounds</h4><p>As the classifier becomes harder to evade with each round, a fixed training budget yields progressively fewer adversarial samples, so we increase the number of policy updates as AT progresses. Empirically the number of new adversarial samples collapses after five rounds under a fixed budget, whereas doubling the policy updates of REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> every five rounds maintains adversarial sample production across all 15 rounds.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>AT-REPLICANT and AT-APG follow the pipeline, however, AT-APG collects adversarial samples during its per-sample rollouts as it does not have a training phase.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Robustness Evaluation</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We evaluate each classifier trained by both AT pipelines against both REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> and APG<sub>WB</sub> attacks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b class="red">9.9%</b><span>mean ASR of REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> against AT-REPLICANT after 15 rounds</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>16.3%</b><span>mean ASR of APG<sub>WB</sub>, an unseen attacker, against AT-REPLICANT</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>62.4%</b><span>mean ASR of REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> against AT-APG, which remains vulnerable</span></div>
    </div>

    <figure class="figure chart" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <figcaption style="margin:0 0 4px">
        <p class="chart-title">Attack success against hardened detectors after 15 rounds</p>
        <p class="chart-sub">Mean ASR (%) over seven detectors, Drebin features. Lower is better for the defender.</p>
      </figcaption>
      <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true"><span><i class="sw-a"></i>Hardened with AT-REPLICANT</span><span><i class="sw-n"></i>Hardened with AT-APG</span></div>
