    <div class="prose">
      <p>After 15 rounds, AT-REPLICANT reduces the mean ASR to 9.9% and 16.3% for REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> and APG<sub>WB</sub>, respectively. Therefore, AT-REPLICANT produces classifiers that are robust against both the attack it was trained on and an unseen attacker. Conversely, AT-APG primarily reduces the mean ASR of APG<sub>WB</sub> (35.6%) while remaining vulnerable to REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> (62.4%).</p>
      <p>APG is greedy and deterministic, thus each round naturally focuses on a small sub-set of capabilities and limits the diversity of adversarial samples produced. As a result, REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> learns capability sequences outside from the targeted paths AT-APG trained on, and achieves a mean ASR of 62.4%. Conversely, the robustness learned by AT-REPLICANT generalizes better as it collects diverse adversarial samples during stochastic policy training, providing significantly more coverage of attack paths. Therefore, the coverage of possible paths modulates how broadly the robustness gained by AT can generalize.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Clean Performance</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>AT-REPLICANT has a more adverse effect than AT-APG to the initial F<sub>1</sub> performance in the first test month (mean F<sub>1</sub> drop across classifiers of −5.6 versus −3.1). However, when considering performance-over-time, both AT pipelines increase mean A-AUT(F<sub>1</sub>) in four of the seven total classifiers, with AT-REPLICANT hardened classifiers improving mean A-AUT(F<sub>1</sub>) by 2.8 compared to the 1.1 gain of AT-APG. Therefore, although AT reduces initial performance, the new malware variants for training created by AT-REPLICANT mildly improve the generalization of most classifiers as samples start to drift over time.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Capability Drift</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In this section we test whether the robustness learned by the AT pipelines generalizes to unseen problem-space capabilities, which we refer to as <span class="term">capability drift</span>. Therefore, we split the capability set in two: (i) the capability set used by both the AT and the initial attacker; and, (ii) the held-out attacker capabilities used to measure generalization to capability drift. Attackers are evaluated six separate times, starting with the initial capability set, and then progressively adding 10% of the total capabilities back in each subsequent evaluation.</p>
      <p>AT-REPLICANT shows robustness against both REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> (8.7%) and APG<sub>WB</sub> (14.6%), whereas AT-APG primarily against APG<sub>WB</sub> (26.6% versus the 49.5% of REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub>). However, as capability drift increases so does the mean ASR of REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub>, with the full capability set reaching 91.3% (AT-REPLICANT) and 96.1% (AT-APG).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table wrap-head">
      <thead><tr><th>Hardened with</th><th class="n">REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> ASR, reduced set (50%)</th><th class="n">REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> ASR, full set (100%)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="hl"><td>AT-REPLICANT</td><td class="n">8.7%</td><td class="n">91.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AT-APG</td><td class="n">49.5%</td><td class="n">96.1%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p style="margin-top:16px">Therefore, as the attacker set changes, the two AT pipelines demonstrate similar robustness to APG<sub>WB</sub>; however, AT-REPLICANT remains consistently more robust than AT-APG to the REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> attacker at every stage of capability drift (mean ASR reduction of −40.8 to −4.9 percentage points). This demonstrates that although AT-REPLICANT generalizes better to capability drift than AT-APG, AT has natural limitations with respect to the capability set it was trained against. Therefore, in practical deployments just as classifiers need active learning to adapt to temporal drift, AT must also adapt to capability drift.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Temporal Drift</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Over time both goodware and malware evolve, without adaptation, the performance of a trained detector decays as new samples drift further from the training distribution. We compare five different pipelines for the classifiers against fixed REPLICANT attackers (under both the white-box and black-box settings): (i) Base, the static classifier from its respective training period; (ii) AL, which performs monthly AL updates with 400 samples selected from that period using uncertainty-based selection; (iii) AT, the AT-REPLICANT pipeline; (iv) AT&amp;AL, the AT-REPLICANT hardened classifiers with AL across the test periods; and, (v) Robust AL (RAL). We propose RAL, which extends AL by running one AT-REPLICANT round on the malware collected each AL period and adds the resulting adversarial samples to the AL update set.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Pipeline</th><th class="n">A-AUT(F<sub>1</sub>) ↑</th><th class="n">A-AUT(ASR), WB ↓</th><th class="n">A-AUT(ASR), BB ↓</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Base (no updates)</td><td class="n">26.7</td><td class="n">99.9</td><td class="n">93.9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AL</td><td class="n">49.8</td><td class="n">98.2</td><td class="n">88.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AT (AT-REPLICANT)</td><td class="n">29.5</td><td class="n">0.1</td><td class="n">0.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AT&amp;AL</td><td class="n">47.9</td><td class="n">19.4</td><td class="n">20.6</td></tr>
        <tr class="hl"><td>RAL (ours)</td><td class="n">49.0</td><td class="n">0.6</td><td class="n">0.8</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p style="margin-top:16px">Drift adaptation (↑A-AUT(F<sub>1</sub>)) and adversarial robustness (↓A-AUT(ASR) WB &amp; BB) are largely orthogonal, with no prior strategy improving both metrics over the three-year evaluation. AL recovers the most clean performance of any configuration (49.8) but, as it never exposes the classifiers to adversarial samples in training, the adaptation only has a minor reduction on attack performance (98.2% WB, 88.5% BB); AT conversely suppresses the attackers to near-zero ASR, however, provides only minor robustness to temporal drift (29.5). Combining AT&amp;AL improves performance with regards to temporal drift at the cost of adversarial robustness (19.4% WB, 20.6% BB). Finally, only our RAL, with its combination of clean and adversarial samples for retraining, improves on both axes, matching the clean performance of AL (49.0 versus 49.8) while preserving the adversarial robustness from AT (0.6% WB versus 0.1% WB).</p>
      <p>Therefore, just as AL is needed to adapt to temporal drift, AT must continually adapt alongside it, as neither AL nor AT alone is sufficient.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="takeaways">
    <h2>Contributions</h2>
    <ul class="takeaways">
      <li><span><strong>REPLICANT Framework.</strong> We propose REPLICANT, a general DRL-based framework that learns problem-space evasion over a capability set as a hierarchical policy, improving Attack Success Rate (ASR) by 20.9%–39.2% compared to state-of-the-art Android attacks.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Policy Transfer.</strong> We demonstrate that a policy trained against a surrogate transfers more effectively than the samples that evade the same surrogate, yielding an 82.0% relative increase in ASR.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>AT &amp; Capability Drift.</strong> We show that AT-REPLICANT reduces attacker success below 17% for both REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub> and a APG<sub>WB</sub>, whereas AT-APG leaves the model over five times more vulnerable to REPLICANT<sub>WB</sub>. We also show that the robustness gained from AT diminishes as the attacker capabilities expand from the capability set used by the defender.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Robustness under Drift.</strong> We establish that applying AL directly to AT hardened classifiers recovers drift-induced performance but degrades robustness, whereas our Robust AL (RAL) retains the performance-over-time of AL and the robustness of AT.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Comprehensive Study.</strong> We conduct the largest evaluation of problem-space Android malware evasion to date, comprising 379,680 attack evaluations, isolating the gains of evasion strategies on attacker success.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote class="excerpt"><p>Together, these results establish learned policies as a state-of-the-art foundation for both attacking and adversarially training malware detectors.</p></blockquote>
  </section>
</Paper>

